    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "Design and execute a numerical experiment to test the Empirical Concentration Hypothesis on small feedforward networks. We will sweep constraint sample size w and hidden layer width N to determine N_c(w), the minimum width achieving zero violation (max abs error ≤ 1e-3) on held-out constraints. This tests whether solution space is rigid (N_c plateaus at small finite value as w increases) or fat (N_c grows unbounded or is unattainable within scan range). We will use a well-defined function class (e.g., smooth 1D/2D analytic functions with known constraints), train small MLPs, and measure generalization error on held-out points. The prediction is that for rigid systems, N_c(w) will plateau at a small finite value (e.g., ≤32) as w increases from 5 to higher values, while for fat systems N_c will be unattainable or grow with w."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
